WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.389 --> 00:00:01.688
- [Film Crew Member] Do we have sound speed?

00:00:01.688 --> 00:00:02.521
- [Film Crew Member] Sound rolling.

00:00:02.521 --> 00:00:03.354
- Ready, Kirk?
- Okay.

00:00:03.354 --> 00:00:05.211
- [Film Crew Member] Roll picture.

00:00:05.211 --> 00:00:07.381
(old-fashioned music)

00:00:07.381 --> 00:00:08.781
- The Smithsonian's National Museum

00:00:08.781 --> 00:00:12.723
of Natural History has
over 144 million objects.

00:00:12.723 --> 00:00:14.333
Like this narwhal tusk.

00:00:14.333 --> 00:00:16.893
(old-fashioned music)

00:00:16.893 --> 00:00:17.984
My name's Kirk Johnson.

00:00:17.984 --> 00:00:20.401
I'm in charge of all of them.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:23.690
99 percent of the collections
are behind the scenes,

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.748
and these are the people
that work with them.

00:00:25.748 --> 00:00:29.665
This is Carla Dove, from
the Division of Birds.

00:00:30.628 --> 00:00:31.839
- [Carla] Here in the Division of Birds

00:00:31.839 --> 00:00:36.758
we have somewhere around
620,000 museum specimens.

00:00:36.758 --> 00:00:38.770
And that represents about 85 percent

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:40.970
of the diversity of birds in the world.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:42.988
You look at these specimens
and you realize that

00:00:42.988 --> 00:00:45.708
this is where the science
is really gonna start.

00:00:45.708 --> 00:00:47.930
We're using our collections
to identify birds

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:48.980
that collide with airplanes,

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.620
and help us improve aviation safety.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:52.820
- [Kirk] Dr. Bob Robbins,

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.020
curator of butterflies and moths.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.842
- [Bob] I have been
professionally employed

00:00:57.842 --> 00:01:01.439
as a lepidopterist who
works on butterflies

00:01:01.439 --> 00:01:03.022
for about 35 years.

00:01:04.228 --> 00:01:06.388
This is the essence of my being,

00:01:06.388 --> 00:01:08.908
is to know everything about butterflies.

00:01:08.908 --> 00:01:11.991
There's a whole world out there that,

00:01:12.839 --> 00:01:16.468
unless you look very
closely, you don't see.

00:01:16.468 --> 00:01:18.268
And it's a interesting world,

00:01:18.268 --> 00:01:19.884
it's a fascinating world,

00:01:19.884 --> 00:01:24.051
and it is full of practical
value for human beings.

00:01:25.348 --> 00:01:26.719
- This is Ellen Strong,

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:28.386
curator of mollusks.

00:01:29.238 --> 00:01:31.809
- [Ellen] What I love about
this collection is its breadth.

00:01:31.809 --> 00:01:33.987
We estimate that we may have

00:01:33.987 --> 00:01:36.526
as many as 20 million specimens.

00:01:36.526 --> 00:01:38.437
We are preserving

00:01:38.437 --> 00:01:41.443
a record of past life on this planet,

00:01:41.443 --> 00:01:42.952
of present life on this planet,

00:01:42.952 --> 00:01:45.620
and preserving that for the future.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:47.592
I take that responsibility very seriously.

00:01:47.592 --> 00:01:49.133
And providing access,

00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:52.152
and caring for the
specimens for posterity.

00:01:52.152 --> 00:01:53.231
- Dr. Jeff Post,

00:01:53.231 --> 00:01:56.520
curator of the national
gem and mineral collection.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:57.768
- [Jeff] Minerals in this collection

00:01:57.768 --> 00:01:59.269
go from something that formed

00:01:59.269 --> 00:02:01.437
maybe in the last few tens of years

00:02:01.437 --> 00:02:04.618
to others that formed more
than three billion years ago.

00:02:04.618 --> 00:02:06.728
We know right now there
are more than 5,000

00:02:06.728 --> 00:02:08.880
minerals that make up the Earth.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:10.697
And in our collection we probably have

00:02:10.697 --> 00:02:11.806
more than half of those.

00:02:11.806 --> 00:02:13.498
Each one's a little piece of the puzzle.

00:02:13.498 --> 00:02:14.989
We try to put all these things together

00:02:14.989 --> 00:02:17.898
and hopefully, eventually
understand the big picture

00:02:17.898 --> 00:02:20.200
of how the Earth works.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.589
- And Floyd Shockley,

00:02:21.589 --> 00:02:24.422
collection manager for entomology.

00:02:25.272 --> 00:02:26.580
- [Floyd] We have just a little over

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.968
35 million specimens in the collection.

00:02:28.968 --> 00:02:31.508
A third of all of insect life on Earth

00:02:31.508 --> 00:02:33.116
is represented by at least one specimen

00:02:33.116 --> 00:02:34.007
in our collection.

00:02:34.007 --> 00:02:36.156
When you look at a
collection the size of ours,

00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:37.446
you're starting to get to a point

00:02:37.446 --> 00:02:39.966
where you can ask really big questions,

00:02:39.966 --> 00:02:42.064
the questions related to
global climate change,

00:02:42.064 --> 00:02:43.615
habitat destruction.

00:02:43.615 --> 00:02:45.756
And you can only answer
those big questions

00:02:45.756 --> 00:02:47.175
if you have a lot of specimens

00:02:47.175 --> 00:02:49.225
collected over a long period of time

00:02:49.225 --> 00:02:51.527
from a lot of different species.

00:02:51.527 --> 00:02:52.465
- To understand the Earth

00:02:52.465 --> 00:02:54.105
and the living things of the Earth,

00:02:54.105 --> 00:02:55.395
you need to collect those things

00:02:55.395 --> 00:02:57.988
and preserve them and study them.

00:02:57.988 --> 00:03:00.457
And that helps you when you need food,

00:03:00.457 --> 00:03:02.299
or when you have a disease.

00:03:02.299 --> 00:03:04.099
All that stuff arcs back

00:03:04.099 --> 00:03:08.888
to the fact that we have samples
of the whole planet here.

00:03:08.888 --> 00:03:10.836
And that's how we understand the planet.

00:03:10.836 --> 00:03:13.419
(bright music)

00:03:16.997 --> 00:03:19.664
(chiming music)

